## Quick Answer: UK Ecommerce Statistics 2026

- Online sales made up **28.3%** of total UK retail sales in July 2026, down from 29.2% in June, according to the Office for National Statistics (ONS, July 2026 bulletin)
- The UK ecommerce market's total size depends heavily on methodology: Mordor Intelligence values it at **USD 317.33 billion** for 2026 at a 9.72% compound annual growth rate, while a separate comparison of trackers, built on Mordor's earlier USD 265.14 billion 2025 estimate rather than its updated 2026 figure, puts the range as wide as USD 152.5 billion to USD 297 billion, so treat any single headline figure with caution
- Wix Stores leads UK ecommerce software adoption with roughly a quarter of tracked sites, with Shopify close behind, per Statista's BuiltWith-sourced UK tracker (August 2026); WooCommerce sits third
- The average UK and Irish ecommerce conversion rate reached **2.26%** in July 2026, up 16.17% year on year from 1.94%, IRP Commerce's own stated growth figure calculated from unrounded underlying data (IRP Commerce)
- The average cart abandonment rate sits at **70.22%**, based on Baymard Institute's aggregation of 50 separate studies
- Mobile devices accounted for **62.3%** of UK and Irish ecommerce sales in July 2026, against 36.3% desktop and 1.4% tablet (IRP Commerce)
- A 0.1 second improvement in page speed lifted retail conversions by **8.4%** and average order value by 9.2% in a Google-commissioned Deloitte study of 30 million sessions

If you sell online and want these numbers checked against your own store's Search Console and analytics data, [get in touch](/contact/) or read on for the full breakdown and sourcing.

## UK Ecommerce Market Size and Growth

Ask five research firms for the size of the UK ecommerce market and you will get five different answers, not because anyone is wrong, but because "ecommerce" gets measured differently depending on whether a tracker counts marketplace sales, services, subscriptions, or only direct retail transactions through a store's own checkout.

What is not disputed is the direction and the underlying retail context. ONS retail sales data, the UK's official statistical record, shows the proportion of sales made online fell from 29.2% in June 2026 to **28.3% in July 2026**. That is still well above the roughly 20% share online retail held before 2020, and it confirms that close to three in every ten pounds spent in UK shops now goes through a digital checkout rather than a physical till.

On overall market value, Mordor Intelligence puts the UK ecommerce market at **USD 317.33 billion in 2026**, growing at a 9.72% compound annual rate through 2031. Other widely cited estimates land lower: a comparison compiled by Netguru across Mordor Intelligence, IMARC Group, eMarketer, EcommerceDB, and Statista forecasts shows a spread from roughly USD 152.5 billion to USD 297 billion, though that comparison uses Mordor's earlier USD 265.14 billion estimate for 2025 rather than the USD 317.33 billion 2026 figure quoted above, so the two sets of numbers reflect different forecast vintages rather than one directly comparable range. If you are building a business case for ecommerce SEO investment, use the growth rate as your evidence rather than any single absolute figure, since the direction of travel is consistent even where the base number is not.

The practical read for a store owner: the UK online retail channel is still expanding meaningfully faster than retail as a whole, but the market is also more contested than it was five years ago, which is exactly why ranking for commercial, non-brand search terms has become harder even as the underlying opportunity has grown.

## UK Ecommerce Platform Market Share: Shopify, WooCommerce, Magento and BigCommerce

Platform choice shapes what an SEO strategy actually looks like in practice, since Shopify, WooCommerce, and Magento each impose different technical constraints on URL structure, canonicalisation, and page speed.

Statista's UK ecommerce software tracker, sourced from BuiltWith and last updated in August 2026, found **Wix Stores leading UK ecommerce site adoption with roughly a quarter of the market**, with Shopify close behind in second place and WooCommerce Checkout in third. The exact percentage breakdown sits behind Statista's paywall, but the ranking itself is consistent with several independent BuiltWith-based trackers, which put Shopify at somewhere between 22% and 24% of UK ecommerce software installs and WooCommerce at 17% to 19%.

Magento (now Adobe Commerce) plays a smaller but commercially significant role. BuiltWith-tracked data cited by Envisage Digital counts roughly **6,955 UK sites running Magento**, around 6.2% of Magento's global install base, concentrated among larger catalogues and enterprise retailers rather than small independent stores. That matches what shows up in practice: Magento rarely competes with Shopify or WooCommerce on store count, but it dominates among stores running tens of thousands of SKUs.

BigCommerce has a real but harder-to-pin-down UK presence. Independent trackers put the UK at somewhere around 6.5% to 9% of BigCommerce's global customer base by different measures (store count versus enriched customer records), but no tracker currently publishes a clean UK market-share percentage the way they do for the other three platforms, so treat any specific number you see quoted for BigCommerce UK share with more scepticism than the Shopify or WooCommerce figures above.

| Platform | UK position | What drives it |
|---|---|---|
| Wix Stores | Leading UK ecommerce software adoption (~25%, Statista/BuiltWith) | Simplicity for small stores and DIY builders |
| Shopify | Close second (~22-24%, multiple BuiltWith-based trackers) | Managed hosting, fast setup, large app ecosystem |
| WooCommerce | Third (~17-19%) | WordPress flexibility, no platform fees |
| Magento / Adobe Commerce | Small store count, large average catalogue (~6,955 UK sites) | Enterprise catalogues, multi-store, complex B2B |
| BigCommerce | Present but not independently ranked in UK trackers | Headless and API-first builds |

None of this changes what actually needs fixing on any given store. Shopify's forced `/collections/` and `/products/` URL structure creates duplicate content that WooCommerce never has to deal with. WooCommerce's plugin stack creates speed problems Shopify's managed hosting mostly avoids. Magento's layered navigation can generate hundreds of thousands of indexable filter URLs that neither of the other two platforms produce at the same scale. The platform you're on determines which of these statistics matter most to your store specifically.

## Organic Search's Share of Ecommerce Traffic vs Paid

Organic search remains one of the largest traffic sources for ecommerce sites, but its share varies more by product category than most generic statistics roundups admit.

A 2026 benchmark compiled from SEC 10-K disclosures (including Etsy, Reverb, REVOLVE and Aterian), Similarweb and HypeAuditor traffic data across roughly 1,000 of the most-visited domains per vertical found organic search's share of ecommerce traffic ranging from **around 15-25% for premium jewellery and luxury goods up to 35-50% for consumer electronics**, with supplements and health products, and home, kitchen, pet and sporting goods categories sitting in the 30-45% range. Paid search typically ranks as the second-largest channel across every category studied, usually somewhere between 15% and 45% depending on how competitive and high-margin the category is.

The same period saw a real shift in how that traffic behaves once it lands on a search results page. Separate 2026 tracking of organic versus paid click share found paid search advertisements gaining ground on organic results across major verticals between January 2025 and January 2026, with organic click share falling by 11 to 23 percentage points year on year in some categories as paid ad formats expanded on the results page.

Organic traffic's importance varies heavily by category, and a strategy built entirely around ranking position without checking your own category's typical organic share risks over- or under-investing relative to what the channel can realistically deliver. Checking your own Search Console data against a category-appropriate benchmark, rather than a generic "SEO drives X% of ecommerce traffic" statistic, gives a far more accurate read on where the opportunity actually sits.

## Ecommerce Conversion Rate Benchmarks

Conversion rate is where most ecommerce SEO conversations eventually land, because ranking traffic that does not convert is not worth very much.

IRP Commerce's UK and Irish ecommerce benchmark, which tracks live merchant data monthly, recorded an **average conversion rate of 2.26% in July 2026**, up 16.17% year on year from 1.94% in July 2025, IRP Commerce's own published growth figure, calculated from unrounded underlying data rather than the rounded 2.26%/1.94% shown on its dashboard. That is a meaningful improvement, though the same dataset showed overall sales value down 10.56% year on year over the same period, a reminder that a rising conversion rate and falling revenue can happen at the same time when traffic volume drops faster than conversion improves.

Conversion rate also varies enormously by sector. Industry-level 2026 benchmarks show rates ranging from around 5% for arts and crafts down to well under 1% for categories like baby and child products, so comparing your store against a flat UK average is close to meaningless without adjusting for category first.

Cart abandonment remains the other side of the conversion equation. Baymard Institute's aggregation of **50 separate studies puts the average documented cart abandonment rate at 70.22%**, a figure that has stayed remarkably stable across the years Baymard has tracked it, even as checkout technology has improved. Extra costs shown too late in checkout, forced account creation, and a checkout process that takes too many steps remain the most commonly cited reasons shoppers abandon a cart before completing a purchase.

## Mobile Commerce in the UK

Mobile is now the primary ecommerce channel for most UK stores.

IRP Commerce's July 2026 data put **mobile at 62.3% of UK and Irish ecommerce sales by device**, against 36.3% for desktop and just 1.4% for tablet. Other trackers put mobile's share of transactions slightly lower, in the 55-57% range. IRP Commerce's page does not specify whether its 62.3% figure is based on transaction count or revenue value, so this page can't confirm the reason for the gap between the two figures: a transaction-count-versus-revenue split is one plausible explanation, but desktop baskets running larger on average would, if anything, be expected to pull a revenue-based mobile share below its transaction-count share, not above it, so that explanation doesn't fully hold up either. What is consistent across every dataset is that mobile now generates the clear majority of UK ecommerce traffic and a majority, or close to it, of sales.

That has direct SEO consequences. Google's mobile-first indexing means the mobile version of a product or category page is what gets crawled and ranked. Core Web Vitals performance still lags on mobile relative to desktop: 48-49% of mobile sites pass Core Web Vitals as of 2026, according to HTTP Archive's Chrome UX Report data, against a meaningfully higher desktop pass rate. A store that has never audited its mobile page speed separately from desktop is very likely leaving both rankings and conversions on the table, since page speed's impact on conversion is not symmetrical between devices.

The evidence for that impact is specific rather than anecdotal. A Google-commissioned study by Deloitte and 55, analysing **30 million user sessions across 37 retail, travel and luxury brands**, found that a 0.1 second improvement in page load speed increased retail conversions by 8.4% and average order value by 9.2%. Speed on product pages mattered most: the study found a 9.1% increase in the rate shoppers progressed from a product page to adding an item to their basket when that page loaded faster. For a mobile-majority store, that means product page speed on a phone is arguably the single most valuable technical fix available.

## What This Means for Your Ecommerce SEO Strategy in 2026

Pull the numbers above together and three priorities stand out for any UK online store for the rest of 2026.

**Fix the platform-specific technical debt first.** Whatever platform you're on, the data above points to a predictable set of problems. Shopify stores lose crawl budget and ranking signal to duplicate `/collections/` URLs. WooCommerce stores carry plugin bloat that drags down Core Web Vitals scores. Magento catalogues generate layered-navigation URL bloat that swamps crawl budget on stores with large SKU counts. My [ecommerce SEO consultant](/services/ecommerce-seo-consultant/) service starts every engagement with a platform-specific technical audit rather than a generic crawl report, because a Shopify store and a Magento store rarely need the same fix. If you're specifically on Shopify, my [Shopify SEO consultant](/services/shopify-seo-consultant/) service addresses the collection URL duplication and app-bloat speed issues that show up on almost every store I audit. WooCommerce owners dealing with thin attribute archives and plugin-driven slowdowns are exactly who my [WooCommerce SEO consultant](/services/woocommerce-seo-consultant/) service is built for, and larger catalogues running Magento's layered navigation are the core of my [Magento SEO consultant](/services/magento-seo-consultant/) work.

**Treat mobile speed as a revenue lever, not a technical checkbox.** With mobile carrying 62.3% of UK ecommerce sales and only around half of mobile sites passing Core Web Vitals, and with a verified 8.4% conversion lift from a 0.1 second speed improvement, product page speed on mobile is one of the few technical changes with a direct, measurable line to revenue rather than just rankings.

**Match your organic investment to your category's actual traffic mix.** If you sell consumer electronics or home goods, organic search plausibly accounts for a third to half of your traffic and deserves proportionate investment. If you sell premium jewellery or luxury goods, organic's typical share is lower and paid or brand channels may carry more of the weight. Benchmarking against your own Search Console data, segmented the way your category actually behaves, beats benchmarking against a flat industry average every time.

None of these numbers replace looking at your own store's data. They give you a UK-specific, sourced starting point for the conversation, whether that's with a developer, an agency, or your own team.

